Use Node.js’s built-in `http` module with the `socketPath` option:

```js
const http = require('node:http');

const req = http.request({
  socketPath: '/tmp/static0.sock',
  path: '/volumes/list',
  method: 'GET',
}, (res) => {
  console.log('STATUS:', res.statusCode);

  res.setEncoding('utf8');
  res.on('data', (chunk) => {
    console.log(chunk);
  });
});

req.on('error', console.error);
req.end();
```

For Unix-domain-socket requests, use `socketPath` instead of `host` and `port`. The request path is specified separately with `path`.

Node.js’s built-in proxy settings are ignored when the request is made to a Unix-domain socket.
